<h2 id="if-there-is-no-response">What happens if it does not work, and what else you may be sold</h2>

<p>Ask this directly, and notice how comfortable the answer sounds. A proportion of people do not respond to PRP. That is not a failing of any particular clinic; it is true of the treatment, and it should be said before the first session rather than after the fourth.</p>

<p>The right response to no response is to stop and change the plan: towards medical treatment if it is not already running, or towards a different conversation if the examination points there. <a href="/blog/prp-or-hair-transplant">Whether you need PRP or a transplant</a> is often that conversation. In practice, stopping is only possible if you have not already paid for sessions you have not had.</p>

<h3>Package pressure worth recognising</h3>

<p>PRP is widely sold as a prepaid block of six or ten sessions. The discount is real. The problem is that it removes your ability to act on a poor review, and removes the clinic's reason to raise one. Treat any of these as a signal to slow down:</p>

<ul>
  <li>A discount that expires today, or applies only if you book the block before leaving</li>
  <li>A session count fixed before anyone has examined your scalp</li>
  <li>Injections offered the same day as the consultation, before indicated blood results are back</li>
  <li>No written terms on what happens to unused sessions if you stop</li>
  <li>PRP, GFC, mesotherapy and supplements bundled into a single plan with a single price</li>
</ul>

<p>None of these proves bad faith on its own. Together they describe a sale arranged around the clinic's cash flow rather than around your review date.</p>

<h3>GFC, exosomes and other upgrades</h3>

<p>Once a PRP price is on the table, the next offer is often a higher tier. GFC, growth factor concentrate, is made from your own blood with an extra processing step that releases the growth factors and removes the cells. It is a legitimate variant, but its published evidence is smaller and newer than PRP's, and it has not been shown to outperform it. The <a href="/gfc-treatment-in-gurgaon">GFC treatment page</a> sets out the same evidence position, and any clinic charging more for GFC should be able to explain why.</p>

<p>Exosome products are different in kind. They are not made from your blood but manufactured from cultured cells, and they have the thinnest evidence of the three. If one is offered, ask what the product is, what cells it came from, who makes it and under what regulatory approval it is being used. <a href="/blog/prp-vs-gfc-vs-exosomes">How PRP, GFC and exosomes compare</a> sets out why those questions matter.</p>

<p>Mesotherapy is a separate issue, because the word describes a way of delivering something rather than what is delivered. If it is added, ask exactly what is in the syringe; <a href="/hair-mesotherapy-in-gurgaon">hair mesotherapy</a> explains why. And when several injectables are given together, nobody can tell afterwards which of them did anything, so you lose the information you would need to drop one.</p>

<h2 id="a-checklist-to-take-with-you">A checklist to take with you</h2>

<ol>
  <li>What is causing my hair loss, and which tests support that?</li>
  <li>Who assesses me, who prepares the PRP, and who injects it, by name?</li>
  <li>Which system is used, single or double spin, activated or not, and is it identical at every session?</li>
  <li>How much is injected, and across which area?</li>
  <li>How many sessions, how far apart, and what is the maintenance plan?</li>
  <li>When is the review, and what will be measured?</li>
  <li>What does the price include and exclude, in writing?</li>
  <li>If the review shows no change, what happens to the plan and to any money already paid?</li>
</ol>

<p>A clinic that answers all eight without hesitating is one you can compare against another. PRP also usually sits alongside, not in place of, <a href="/blog/minoxidil-and-finasteride-in-india">the prescription medicines for pattern hair loss</a>, so expect that conversation too. <a href="/hair-prp-treatment-in-gurgaon">How PRP is run at our Sector 39 clinic</a> sets out the process step by step, and a consultation there starts with the diagnosis rather than the package.</p>

